data: {"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"### 29215ab3-7245-51aa-baee-1b98d01b3af5\n`eip-7702@final` · repo:0603514569547869ae2531a4e5b2b62875888db3 · as_of 2026-08-18T11:59:19Z\nhttps://github.com/ethereum/EIPs/blob/0603514569547869ae2531a4e5b2b62875888db3/EIPS/eip-7702.md#L498-L514\n\n```\nSecurity Considerations › Implementation of secure delegate contracts\nThe following is a non-exhaustive list of pitfalls that delegate contracts\n*should* be wary of and require a signature over from the account's authority:\n\n* Replay protection (e.g., a nonce) should be implemented by the delegate…\n```\n\n### c465eade-6a90-53ac-a48a-f7fc6daf03af\n`eip-7702@final` · repo:0603514569547869ae2531a4e5b2b62875888db3 · as_of 2026-08-18T11:59:19Z\nhttps://github.com/ethereum/EIPs/blob/0603514569547869ae2531a4e5b2b62875888db3/EIPS/eip-7702.md#L262-L277\n\n```\nRationale › General design philosophy › Interaction with applications and wallets\nWhile this EIP provides a lot of flexibility to applications and EOAs, there are\nincorrect ways of using it. Applications **must not** expect that they can\nsuggest the user sign an authorization, and therefore it is…\n```\n\n### 0e4cfe8e-1522-5288-94c9-f107d38ba5a1\n`eip-7702@final` · repo:0603514569547869ae2531a4e5b2b62875888db3 · as_of 2026-08-18T11:59:19Z\nhttps://github.com/ethereum/EIPs/blob/0603514569547869ae2531a4e5b2b62875888db3/EIPS/eip-7702.md#L447-L463\n\n```\nRationale › Rationale for technical details › No blobs, no contract creation\nTransactions should be thought of as specialized tools and not necessarily a\none-type-does-all solution. EIP-4844 is treated differently at the p2p level due\nto burden blobs place on a node's bandwidth. EIP-7702 has…\n```\n\n### 78743079-9e46-5536-acae-55e97e6c95b6\n`eip-7702@final` · repo:0603514569547869ae2531a4e5b2b62875888db3 · as_of 2026-08-18T11:59:19Z\nhttps://github.com/ethereum/EIPs/blob/0603514569547869ae2531a4e5b2b62875888db3/EIPS/eip-7702.md#L279-L297\n\n```\nRationale › General design philosophy › Forward-compatibility with future account abstraction\nThis EIP is designed to be forward-compatible with endgame account abstraction,\nwithout over-enshrining any fine-grained details of [ERC-4337](./eip-4337.md) or\nRIP-7560.\n\nTo start, the `address` that…\n```\n\n### 6e35b641-5075-563b-8909-8ab81320917c\n`eip-7702@final` · repo:0603514569547869ae2531a4e5b2b62875888db3 · as_of 2026-08-18T11:59:19Z\nhttps://github.com/ethereum/EIPs/blob/0603514569547869ae2531a4e5b2b62875888db3/EIPS/eip-7702.md#L343-L348\n\n```\nRationale › General design philosophy › Self-sponsoring: allowing `tx.origin` to set code\n* Set `tx.origin` to a constant `ENTRY_POINT` address when using the `CALL*`\ninstruction in the context of an EOA.\n* Set `tx.origin` to a special address derived from the sender or signer\naddresses.\n*…\n```\n\n### 74c9946e-5215-58ea-b896-202c06f8492d\n`eip-7702@final` · repo:0603514569547869ae2531a4e5b2b62875888db3 · as_of 2026-08-18T11:59:19Z\nhttps://github.com/ethereum/EIPs/blob/0603514569547869ae2531a4e5b2b62875888db3/EIPS/eip-7702.md#L465-L471\n\n```\nRationale › Rationale for technical details › Disallow delegation to precompiles\nPrecompiles are themselves edge cases, so allowing delegations to precompiles or\nnot requires some focus in implementation. Considering the fact that precompiles\ntechnically do not have code associated with their…\n```\n\n### c4af5c4e-c687-58bf-824c-6c63132b92fd\n`eip-7702@final` · repo:0603514569547869ae2531a4e5b2b62875888db3 · as_of 2026-08-18T11:59:19Z\nhttps://github.com/ethereum/EIPs/blob/0603514569547869ae2531a4e5b2b62875888db3/EIPS/eip-7702.md#L321-L341\n\n```\nRationale › General design philosophy › Self-sponsoring: allowing `tx.origin` to set code\nExamples of (1) and (2) can be found in contracts deployed on Ethereum mainnet,\nwith (1) being more common (and unaffected by this proposal). On the other hand,\nuse case (3) is more severely affected by this…\n```\n\n### fc145e56-8844-5faf-8479-b2799a8de860\n`eip-7702@final` · repo:0603514569547869ae2531a4e5b2b62875888db3 · as_of 2026-08-18T11:59:19Z\nhttps://github.com/ethereum/EIPs/blob/0603514569547869ae2531a4e5b2b62875888db3/EIPS/eip-7702.md#L299-L319\n\n```\nRationale › General design philosophy › Self-sponsoring: allowing `tx.origin` to set code\nAllowing `tx.origin` to set code and execute its own delegated code enables what\nis called self-sponsoring. It allows users to take advantage of EIP-7702 without\nrelying on any third party…\n```"}],"structuredContent":{"intent":"lookup","recipe":null,"hits":[{"selector":"29215ab3-7245-51aa-baee-1b98d01b3af5","source_url":"https://github.com/ethereum/EIPs/blob/0603514569547869ae2531a4e5b2b62875888db3/EIPS/eip-7702.md#L498-L514","snippet":"Security Considerations › Implementation of secure delegate contracts\nThe following is a non-exhaustive list of pitfalls that delegate contracts\n*should* be wary of and require a signature over from the account's authority:\n\n* Replay protection (e.g., a nonce) should be implemented by the delegate…","project":"eip-7702","version":"final","version_defaulted":false,"last_validated":"2026-08-19T09:14:34Z","as_of":"2026-08-18T11:59:19Z","provenance_kind":"repo","pin_ref":"0603514569547869ae2531a4e5b2b62875888db3"},{"selector":"c465eade-6a90-53ac-a48a-f7fc6daf03af","source_url":"https://github.com/ethereum/EIPs/blob/0603514569547869ae2531a4e5b2b62875888db3/EIPS/eip-7702.md#L262-L277","snippet":"Rationale › General design philosophy › Interaction with applications and wallets\nWhile this EIP provides a lot of flexibility to applications and EOAs, there are\nincorrect ways of using it. Applications **must not** expect that they can\nsuggest the user sign an authorization, and therefore it is…","project":"eip-7702","version":"final","version_defaulted":false,"last_validated":"2026-08-19T09:14:34Z","as_of":"2026-08-18T11:59:19Z","provenance_kind":"repo","pin_ref":"0603514569547869ae2531a4e5b2b62875888db3"},{"selector":"0e4cfe8e-1522-5